meta {
  name: Search treats a wildcard as a character
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/catalog/products?q=%25&pageSize=1
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  The one catalog parameter the collection never exercised, and the reason the README used
  to say this API had no server-side search: nobody was looking for it.

  `q` runs an escaped ILIKE over name and description. A bare `%` in LIKE matches any run of
  characters, so an unescaped term turns the search box into a query language - not an
  injection, since the term has always been a parameter, but the difference between a search
  that filters and one that does not.

  Searching for a literal `%` against a generated catalog whose names contain no percent sign
  therefore has exactly one correct answer: nothing. Without the escaping the same request
  returns every product there is, which is what the totals below tell apart.
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.total: eq 0
}

tests {
  test("a lone wildcard finds nothing rather than everything", function() {
    // The generated catalog has no percent sign in any name or description, so this is the
    // whole assertion: escaped it matches nothing, unescaped it matches all 288 products.
    expect(res.body.items).to.be.an("array").that.is.empty;
    expect(res.body.total).to.equal(0);
    expect(Number(bru.getVar("catalogTotal"))).to.be.above(0);
  });
}
